  "generated_text": "1. We know the total number of slices is 12.\n2. We know that 6 slices have pepperoni.\n3. We know that 10 slices have mushrooms.\n4. We need to find the number of slices that have both pepperoni and mushrooms.\n\nLet's use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to solve this problem.\n\nLet \\( P \\) be the number of slices with pepperoni, \\( M \\) be the number of slices with mushrooms, and \\( B \\) be the number of slices with both pepperoni and mushrooms.\n\nWe know:\n- \\( P = 6 \\)\n- \\( M = 10 \\)\n- Total slices = 12\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion-exclusion:\n\\[ P + M - B = \\text{Total slices} \\]\n\nSubstitute the known values:\n\\[ 6 + 10 - B = 12 \\]\n\nSolve for \\( B \\):\n\\[ 16 - B = 12 \\]\n\\[ 16 - 12 = B \\]\n\\[ B = 4 \\]\n\nSo, the answer is:\n\\(\\boxed{4}\\)",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[4, '4']",
    "gold_answer": "There are 12 total slices, and 6 of them have pepperoni and 10 have mushrooms. Let $n$ be the number of the slices that have both toppings, so there are $6-n$ with just pepperoni and $10-n$ with just mushrooms.  Since every slice has at least one topping, and there are 12 slices, we must have $(6-n) + (10-n) + n = 12$, which gives $n=\\boxed{4}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
